[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k3/8/4K3/4P3/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ Time to learn pawn endings. Our first word is opposition. }
1... Kd7 { Kings nose to nose, and he had to move. He steps left, so you step right. [%cal Ye5f6] } 2. Kf6 { That is outflanking. Your king gets past him and heads for the queening square. [%csl Ge8] } (2. Kd5 { Opposition again, and it also wins. But stepping across was quicker. } 2... Ke7 { He faces you once more and all the work is still to come. }) (2. Kf5 { Also winning, but sideways is slow. Your king wants to go forwards. } 2... Ke7 { He is back in front of the pawn and you have spent a move. }) (2. Kd4 { Never step backwards in front of your own pawn. } 2... Kd6 { He takes the opposition and your king can never get past. The win is gone. }) (2. Kf4 { The same retreat, the same trouble. } 2... Ke6 { He stands in front of the pawn and holds it forever. Only a draw. }) 2... Kd8 { Straight back is his best defence. Now take the square beside him. [%cal Yf6f7] } 3. Kf7 { The queening square is yours. The pawn simply walks home now. [%csl Ge8][%cal Ge4e8] } (3. e5 { The pawn is in a hurry. It still wins, but let the king lead. } 3... Ke8 { He sits in front of it and you must find the king moves anyway. }) (3. Ke6 { Also winning. But f7 grabs the queening square at once. } 3... Kc7 { He runs to the other wing and you have more walking to do. }) (3. Kf5 { Still winning, but you have undone your own good move. } 3... Ke7 { He is in front of the pawn again. Start over. }) (3. Ke5 { In front of your own pawn, and the win is gone. } 3... Ke7 { He has the opposition and holds the draw. }) 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: do not push too soon"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/rquahaDY"]
[FEN "5k2/8/5K2/6P1/8/8/8/8 b - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/8/5K2/6P1/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_1"]

{ King in front, pawn behind, and it is his move. Exactly one move wins. }
1... Kg8 { He hides in the corner. Take your time with this one. } 2. Kg6 { Opposition. Now he must give way and your king walks in. [%csl Gg6,Gg8] } (2. g6 { The pawn runs first, and that throws the whole win away. } 2... Kf8 3. g7+ Kg8 { His king sits in front of the pawn forever. Drawn. [%csl Rg8] }) (2. Ke6 { Stepping across worked last time. It does not work with a knight pawn. } 2... Kg7 { He gets in front of the pawn and it can never pass. Drawn. }) (2. Ke7 { Same idea, same problem: you have left the g column. } 2... Kg7 { His king blocks the pawn and the win is gone. }) (2. Ke5 { Backwards, and the pawn is left on its own. } 2... Kf7 { He walks straight at it and holds the draw. }) (2. Kf5 { A step back and the opposition goes with it. } 2... Kf7 { He faces you, and your pawn will never queen. }) 2... Kf8 { He steps aside. Where does your king want to be? } 3. Kh7 { Perfect. The g pawn walks home and nobody can stop it. [%csl Gg8][%cal Gg5g8] } (3. Kf6 { Also winning, but you have walked in a circle. } 3... Kg8 { Back where you started. The corner square was the one. }) (3. Kh6 { Also winning, just slower. } 3... Kg8 { He is in the corner again and you still have to find h7. }) (3. Kf5 { Your king lets go, and the win goes with it. } 3... Kf7 { He faces you and the pawn can never pass. Drawn. }) (3. Kh5 { Backwards again, and now he simply blocks. } 3... Kg7 { In front of the pawn, and it is a draw. }) 1-0


[Event "Pawn endgames: go the long way round"]
[Result "1-0"]
