{ You are black now, a pawn up, and his king is coming. One move wins. }
1. Ke2 { He steps in front of your pawn. This move decides the game. } 1... c3 { Push! Now his king can never use the square in front of your pawn. [%csl Gc2] } (1... Kd4 { The king first, and that hands him the time he needs. } 2. Kd2 { His king sits in front of the pawn and it can never move. Drawn. }) (1... Kf4 { You walk away from your own pawn. } 2. Kd2 { He blocks it, and your pawn is stuck there forever. }) (1... Kd5 { Too slow. Here the pawn has to lead. } 2. Kd2 { Straight in front of the pawn, and there is nothing left to play for. }) (1... Ke5 { Backwards, and the moment is gone. } 2. Kd2 { He takes c2 away from your pawn. Drawn. }) 2. Ke1 { He drops back to the last rank. Which square do you want? } 2... Ke3 { Opposition, and now his king has to let your pawn through. [%csl Ge3,Ge1][%cal Gc3c1] } (2... Kd4 { Also winning, but you have given him a free move. } 3. Ke2 { He is still fighting. The opposition on e3 was quicker. }) 0-1


[Event "Pawn endgames: the rule of the square"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/J7JDkozw/nG3HbTy0"]
