{ Four from real games now, and no hints. First one: your c pawn is attacked. Count before you defend. }
40... Kc6 { He steps up to win the c pawn. Is that pawn really the important thing? } 41. Ke5 { Let him have it. Your king is running for his pawns and it is faster. [%csl Rc4] } (41. Kd3 { You defend the pawn and the game with it. } 41... Kd6 42. Kd4 Ke6 { Nobody can make progress. Drawn. }) (41. Ke4 { Keeping the king in the middle, but the race needed starting now. } 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 { He shuffles and holds. Drawn. }) (41. Ke3 { Backwards, and the race is over before it began. } 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 { Nothing to do. Drawn. }) (41. Kc3 { The same waiting move on the other side. } 41... Kc5 42. Kd3 Kc6 { A draw. }) (41. f5 { Breaking on the wing first, and his king walks over. } 41... Kd6 42. f6 Kc6 { He holds everything. The win is gone. }) (41. c5 { Pushing the pawn he was about to take anyway. } 41... b5 42. Kd3 Kxc5 { He is a pawn up and you are losing. }) 41... Kc5 { He goes for the pawn. Keep running. } 42. Kf6 { His king is on the wrong side of the board now. } (42. f5 { A trade, and it hands everything back. } 42... Kxc4 43. fxg6 fxg6 { The pawns come off and it is a draw. }) (42. Ke4 { Turning back to defend. Far too slow. } 42... Kxc4 43. Kf3 b5 { His pawn runs and yours never queens. Lost. }) 42... Kxc4 { He has his pawn. You are about to take two. } 43. Kxf7 { One given, two taken, and the g pawn is next. That is how you count a race. [%csl Gg6] } (43. f5 { A trade at the wrong moment. } 43... gxf5 44. Kxf7 f4 { Both sides queen. Dead level. }) (43. Kg7 { Round the other way, and it costs the tempo you needed. } 43... b5 44. Kxf7 b4 { Both sides queen now. The win has gone. }) (43. Ke7 { The same move too many. } 43... b5 44. Kxf7 b4 { One move too slow, and it is only a draw. }) (43. Ke5 { Back towards his king, and the race turns against you. } 43... b5 44. Kf6 b4 { His pawn queens and you are lost. }) 1-0


