{ Last one. A rook pawn is the fussy kind: your king must own the corner before the pawn runs. }
56... Ke8 { He is running for h8. If he ever gets there this is a draw. } 57. Kg7 { g7. The corner is yours now and his king can never come near it. [%csl Gh8] } (57. h5 { The pawn runs alone and he strolls into the corner. } 57... Kf8 58. h6 Kg8 { He sits on h8 and you can never push him out. Drawn. }) (57. Kg6 { One square short, and he still slips in. } 57... Kf8 58. h5 Kg8 { The corner is his and the game is drawn. }) (57. Kf5 { Backwards, and the road to h8 is wide open. } 57... Ke7 58. h5 Kf7 { He is in front of the pawn. Drawn. }) (57. Ke6 { Chasing his king instead of taking the corner. } 57... Kf8 58. h5 Kg7 { He blocks the pawn forever. }) 57... Ke7 { He tries to come back. Too late for that. } 58. h5 { Now the pawn runs, with your king already sitting on the queening corner. [%cal Gh5h8] } (58. Kg6 { You let go of the corner and he runs straight for it. } 58... Kf8 59. h5 Kg8 { Drawn. }) (58. Kh6 { Sideways, and now his king has time. } 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kf7 { He walks back in. Drawn. }) (58. Kh7 { The wrong corner square, and it lets his king back. } 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kf7 { A draw. }) (58. Kg8 { Backwards, when your king was already perfect. } 58... Kf6 59. h5 Kg5 { He picks up the pawn. Drawn. }) 1-0


